<!--
    Mach-O Core Foundation constant strings (`option cfstrings on|off`).

    Issue: a `CFSTR("x")` compiles to a four-word CFString struct in
    `__cfstring` (isa, flags, char pointer, length). Code references the
    struct, not the characters, so the constant-string route, which reads
    the bytes AT the referenced address, sees the struct and prints the
    entry address. In an 84 MB arm64 third-party iOS app kuna printed
    `NSLog(0x1044376b0)` for `NSLog(CFSTR("Reachability Flag Status: ..."))`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P1 external-refinement, the Mach-O `cfstrings`
    analysis pass (`kuna-analysis/src/analyzers/cfstring/mod.rs`), whose
    facts the commit seam turns into an opaque `struct __CFString` entry,
    whose pointer the type factory spells `CFStringRef`, plus a StringManager
    seed at the entry address (`kuna-console/src/engine.rs`).

    P0 assertion: `option cfstrings on|off|annotate` (default on). It is a commit-time
    gate, so it is set after `load file` and before the `read symbols` that
    commits the analysis.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture. Both passes clear
    it and load the real arm64 Mach-O fixture `macho_cfstring` (built from
    `macho_cfstring.c`, see the fixtures README): three hand-built CFString
    constants in `__DATA_CONST,__cfstring` and a getter returning each one's
    address. `_get_greeting` @0x100000538 returns "cfstring hello" @0x100004008;
    `_get_after` @0x100000550 returns the entry @0x100004048, which sits right
    behind an entry whose string is longer than its own 32 bytes, so it is only
    reached when that entry's type stays inside the entry.

    Pass 1 (`option cfstrings off`, the BUG): the getters return the entries, named by
    their data symbols (`&_kGreeting`, `&_kAfter`), not their strings. Pass 2
    (`option cfstrings on`, the default, the FIX): they return `CFSTR("cfstring hello")` and
    `CFSTR("after the long one")`, typed `CFStringRef`. Pass 3 (`option cfstrings annotate`): the same,
    each followed by a comment naming its entry address.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c0035fd6
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
